<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<assembly manifestVersion="1.0" xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v1">
  <assemblyIdentity version="1.0.0.0" name="Winora.App.app" />
  <trustInfo xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
    <security>
      <requestedPrivileges>
        <!--
          Administrator, asked for before the process starts rather than taken afterwards.

          Winora changes the machine it runs on: the taskbar, File Explorer, cursors, themes, the
          bypass. Much of that needs rights an ordinary token does not carry, and the previous
          arrangement — start plain, notice a locked row, offer a restart — meant a person met the
          program for the first time through a wall of settings it could not touch.

          requireAdministrator rather than relaunching with the runas verb: Windows asks once, up
          front, and there is only ever one process. The relaunch left two of them alive for a
          moment and the window flickered while the second took over.

          The cost, written here because it is easy to forget and expensive to rediscover: an
          elevated window cannot be automated from an ordinary session. UI Automation will not
          attach, PrintWindow returns a blank frame, and neither Stop-Process nor CloseMainWindow
          reaches it. Every screen change from here on has to be verified by hand.
        -->
        <requestedExecutionLevel level="requireAdministrator" uiAccess="false" />
      </requestedPrivileges>
    </security>
  </trustInfo>
  <compatibility xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:compatibility.v1">
    <application>
      <supportedOS Id="{8e0f7a12-bfb3-4fe8-b9a5-48fd50a15a9a}" />
    </application>
  </compatibility>
  <application xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
    <windowsSettings>
      <dpiAwareness xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2016/WindowsSettings">PerMonitorV2</dpiAwareness>
    </windowsSettings>
  </application>
</assembly>
